WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:05.440
Scientists are embarking on a massive effort to CT-scan all vertebrates--

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:08.740
from bats to turtles to squirrels.

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:14.509
Countless animal specimens are preserved and
stored away in museum collections,

00:00:14.509 --> 00:00:18.270
making them difficult for scientists, and the public, to access.

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:22.060
But the plan to “scan all vertebrates”
will change that

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:27.480
by creating 3D models of the animals and uploading them to a free online database

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:33.540
Over the next four years, 16 universities and museums across the United States.

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:37.020
will scan more than 20,000 fluid-preserved animals,

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:40.580
covering about 15,000 different species.

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:45.460
Even though that’s only 22% of the 68,000
vertebrate species,

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:51.040
they will be carefully picked to cover over 80% of living vertebrate genera.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:56.340
To speed things up, researchers have made
“fish burritos,” where multiple specimens,

00:00:56.350 --> 00:01:01.929
such as these piranhas, are wrapped, stacked,
and scanned together.

00:01:01.929 --> 00:01:07.860
This unique view of vertebrates has already
led to discoveries, including new species,

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:13.140
such as this clingfish, that was hiding in
a museum for several years.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:18.649
They can also help scientists understand new
quirks about animal diets and lifestyles,

00:01:18.649 --> 00:01:23.920
such as this Philippine flat-headed frog that
ate a crab before being preserved,

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:31.080
or this other frog that’s so tiny, the ant inside
its belly is almost as big as its head!

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:34.460
Texas A&amp;M will be in charge of scanning anything large.

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:41.130
In the past they’ve scanned an eel, a bowhead whale fetus, and even a shark.

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:45.940
The project will also pick about 1,000 specimens
to soak in an iodine dye,

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:48.720
which boosts the contrast on soft tissues,

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:53.800
improving the view of the muscles, circulatory system, and brain.

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:58.740
“Scan-all-vertebrates” is a basic science
initiative to inspire new research

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:05.680
and is a huge leap forward in digitally preserving, and sharing, biodiversity on Earth.

